the president of ride-sharing company  lifts as the deadly crash in Arizona  involving an uber self-driving SUV could  have been prevented lyft is continuing  trials of its own autonomous vehicles  uber has now decided to stop testing its  driverless cars in California  uber tests are also suspended in Arizona  lifts market share is growing its 2017  revenue crossed the billion dollar  threshold the company says that's up a  hundred sixty eight percent since the  end of 2016  John Zimmer is co-founder and president  of lyft John good morning welcome thanks  for having me any pause about your  ownself driverless vehicles after the  the uber decision I think everyone  should take pause when there's a tragedy  like what happened in Arizona but it's  also important to zoom out and  understand why companies are doing this  and the fact is that 35,000 people every  year are killed in a car accident and  the ultimate goal is to bring that to  zero are you noticing that there's more  of an increase in in lyft mentions you  know before John it used to be uber uber  uber but now I see it's uber and lyft in  the same conversation I see more  cultural references to lifts in movies  and on TV shows so I'm wondering from  your point of view what's working for  lyft I for one I'm glad you got rid of  the pink mustaches but you did a long  time ago but what is working what's  working for you guys I think what's  working well is our vision which is to  improve people's lives with the world's  best transportation the way we treat  people whether that's our drivers or  passengers my background or our team  members or employees my backgrounds in  hospitality and so to me this was always  a service business what does that mean  hospitality like hotels hotels school oh  not technology okay right yeah and I one  of my first Jobs was a phone operator in  a hotel and and I saw the obvious  importance of taking care of people to  provide great service and I think that's  what's really paying off you actually  walked the walk - we should say listen  we always send a very nice car to pick  up our guests and we said we're gonna  send the car you said nope I'm gonna  take a lift I would like to do that I'll  get there on my own  but you also Drive you're also so  somebody can put in an order and you're  the one that picks them up while you do  that it's it's critical that we  understand  hire experience so every New Year's and  I try to every month as well I Drive on  on lifts and we learn a lot yeah I  learned a lot I learned the challenges  of being a driver on the platform the  opportunities for us to improve the  product for our drivers and I understand  better the passenger experience from a  different perspective  why is lyft better than uber lyft is  better than uber because we care about  improving our cities we care about  improving people's lives and we care  about the people that are behind the  business and nuber doesn't you  trillion-dollar market  ride-sharing why isn't eber or lyft  profitable yet the focus right now is on  on growth that the opportunity here is  that americans spend two trillion  dollars every year on car ownership know  we spend more money on cars than we do  on food but we use our car only four  percent of the time and so we're going  after that really large yeah you and  your co-founder Logan green you're  actually pushing for the end of personal  car ownership in major cities by by 2025  I believe it will make financial sense  for someone to own a vehicle in major  cities by 2025 because we spent the  house it won't mix it won't make  financial sense you spent $9,000 every  year owning and operating a vehicle when  you look at all the costs and you use  the car just four percent of the time on  average that does not make sense we can  give the equivalent of a five thousand  dollar tax credit back to every  household if we if we improve that  efficiency and that that's worth doing  oh that's a hard sell for me because  suppose you just want to run to the  store for just a second yeah I got to  wait and dial it in but I mean you're  raising an interesting point here you're  raising an interesting point the other  thing that I think is fascinating about  you and co-founder Logan Green is that  you guys are known as the good guys in  the business in the business world and I  think something that struck me  interesting is that you all just  recently ordered an internal audit last  year a self-imposed internal audit what  were you looking for why did you do that  we did an internal audit around equal  pay and and to me this is just obvious  if someone's obvious that if someone's  doing the same work they should be paid  the same and and we didn't want our team  members our employees to take our word  for it we wanted a third party to  validate that was true so we did that  last year  and I think it was yesterday we  announced our commitment to do that  every year  did you find discrepancies there was  about 0.6 percent discrepancies likely  from some type of unconscious bias or  just different opinions and we addressed  those John Zimmer thanks for being here  we appreciate it thanks for having me  yes good to see you

